    "question": {
      ":question/id": "820d4264-fe4e-5f1b-a987-23a8133e9ff8",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)= (y + 1)\\ln (x - 3),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y + 1)\\ln (x - 3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y + 1}{x - 3} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y + 1)\\ln (x - 3)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x - 3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y + 1}{x - 3} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x - 3) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y + 1}{x - 3} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x - 3) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y + 1}{x - 3}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x - 3)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y + 1=0,$ which means $y=-1.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x - 3)=0,$ which means that $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(4,-1).$",
