      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Given that $f(x,y)= (y - 2)\\ln (x + 1),$ find the points $(x, y)$ where $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, we find the partial derivatives: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}((y - 2)\\ln (x + 1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{y - 2}{x + 1} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}((y - 2)\\ln (x + 1)) \\\\  & =\\ln(x + 1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = \\dfrac {y - 2}{x + 1} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\nabla f(x,y) = \\mathbf 0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {y - 2}{x + 1} \\, \\mathbf i + \\ln(x + 1) \\, \\mathbf j = 0 \\mathbf i + 0\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nBy equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf{i}$ on both sides of the equation (and similarly with $\\mathbf{j}$), we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y - 2}{x + 1}=0 \\\\ \\ln(x + 1)=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $y - 2=0,$ which means $y=2.$ From the second equation, we obtain $\\ln(x + 1)=0,$ which means that $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, $\\nabla f(x,y) =\\mathbf 0$ at the point $(0,2).$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "2cd2698a-3e7a-5c64-a480-b6972d614781",
          ":answer-field/key": "selection",
